Date: Tue, 2 Feb 1993 21:37:58 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: GA and VR

 
Nicolas Berloquin:
> We would have a 3D squeletton (could be cylinders), and the angles
> between each limb (torso, arm, forearm, legs...) would be coded
> in the chromosome. [evolve angles that match image]

Interesting idea.  I suspect the limb lengths would also need to be 
coded.  The fitness function must determine how closely the skeleton 
matches the TV images, so there would still be some image processing 
involved, but it might be simpler than an algorithm that tries to 
directly extract the image.  

Either kind of algorithm does indeed sound compute-intensive, not 
the sort of thing that could respond to user movements in real time.
GAs often take days to run, so even a few orders of magnitude
improvement in the hardware might not be sufficient. The problem 
would be easier if the user wore distinctive clothing or tape that forms 
a skeleton pattern.

You might try bouncing this idea off folks in the newsgroup comp.ai.genetic.
Also, an interesting mailing list is genetic-programming.  In GP the "gene"
is a parse tree, and computer programs are recombined heirarchically instead
of bit strings crossed over linearly.  No reason we should be confined to
nature's limited representation!

Another way to use GA in VR might be an adaptive user interface.  The
genetic code might represent user preferences, like those found in an
X resources file.  The fitness function might determine how quickly
the user can access the most commonly used objects.   This could
operate in the background and update the GUI style every few seconds
or minutes, so it need not be real time.  Alas, the constantly 
changing style of user interface might be quite disconcerting to 
the user.... the GUI would be trying to learn about the user at the 
same time the user was trying to learn the GUI... :-)

