From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Outlawry

(Sorry for the quotes-within-quotes...it's just Perry responding to Nick's
response to Perry's comments.)

>> From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
>> 
>> Perry Metzger:
>> > Certainly there is no central organization dictating here, but remember,
>> > if someone fails to obey a contract that they have themselves signed, its
>> > unlikely any rational security agency will take them on as a client.
>> 
>> What about Wolf's-Head Unlimited, which specializes in protecting the 
>> outlaws of the world? 
>
>Being itself outlawed, its likely that a smart guy with an F15 could collect
>millions in bounties with a single bombing run on their headquarters, and
>no one will notice.
>
>Perry

We don't have to look very far to see what are effectively the "Wolf's-Head
Unlimited" types of protection agencies that Nick was referring to. Namely,
the "Bloods" and the "Crips," not to mention dozens of others protection
agency/criminal organizations.

The Medellin Cartel, the Yakuza, the Russian Mafia, the main Mafia, the
Jamaican gangs, the CIA, etc. Each protects its members to some extent,
through physical protection, hiding, escape routes, bribery, etc.
Naturally, each organization makes certain decisions about how far it will
go to protect its members--sometimes even Wolf's-Head decides to toss one
of its members to the wolves.

Perry's point about the F-15 strike seems a bit simplistic, else the
Columbian drug cartel likely would've been wiped out long ago. Other
constraints factor in, and still would even in a largely stateless world.

Nor does Nick's point refute PPL. I don't think he was trying to--I think
he was just making an excellent point about some possible flies in the
ointment about protection agencies arising to protect outlaws. This, by the
way, was a point David Friedman acknowledged as needing much more research
when we talked to him in Oakland some months back. 

Freidman and Benson both cite societies like Iceland and Ireland (and
Benson also describes a Pacific Island with very similar customs). The case
of an obvious murderer in a relatively small society is fairly ideal for
ostracism and similar approaches.

Much harder to handle are cases of entire gangs of folks who become
outlaws. As others have also noted, the set of nations can in some sense be
considered to be PPL in action. (And obviously Benson's discussion of
Merchant Law and how it arose between nations explicitly makes this point.)
"Ostracism" doesn't always work, for reasons familiar to readers of this
list (defections, smuggling, covert channels, self-sufficiency, etc.)

(Understand that I'm not suggesting that, for example, wars be launched
against nations like Iraq.)

Perhaps looking at how nations cooperated to handle piracy on the high seas
would be useful. (On the other hand, nations often _used_ pirates as
instruments of national policy, perhaps similar to the way the CIA is
alleged to use the drug cartels?)

Anyway, I have no firm ideas on how this problem of outlaws will be
handled, except to doubt the glib claims that they'd be eliminated by the
mere fact of being fair game for anyone.

--Tim May


