Date:     Wed, 3 Feb 93 8:48:16 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: ECON: Medicine & Redistribution

It's true, as Dale pointed out, that most people have private health
insurance without being treated as price-seeking consumers, but this is
the result of some statist interventions.

During WWII, Roosevelt II put price ceilings on wages.  As an attempt to
get better employees, firms began offering non-wage compensation,
including health insurance.  The IRS ruled that this was tax-exempt.  This
ruling has remained in place, and led to most people having their health
insurance through their employer; there's no exemption for personal health
insurance premiums.

Thus, employers are the ones trying to police these costs, not patients.
The solution to this is to replace the status quo with an exemption on
one's personal income tax for health insurance.

Since the subject of international comparison was also brought up by Dale,
consider the policy of Singapore: "provident funds" which everyone is
required to save 6% of their income in to pay for their medical care.
Provident funds are also what Chile has instead of Social Security.

The USA and other countries could easily adapt this: establish tax-free
Medisave accounts where income could be accumulated over the years to
cover medical care expenses.  The money could be earning interest, invested,
used to buy insurance, etc, so long as the only consumer services that
could be purchased with it were medical care services.

Since the exemption would be from personal income, the consumers, patients,
themselves would police costs.  Since payments would be in cash, cash
discounts would be possible.  We needn't be so authoritarian as Chile or
Singapore and force people to save, but need merely allow some to do so;
the resultant price decreases will benefit even those who don't have
Medisave accounts.

As for Garrett's view of the Redistributive State: it seems cheaper to
pay police to keep criminals from stealing one's property than to give it
away to the criminals.  My view is that the beneficiaries of statist
anticompetitive policies are often persuaded to share some of the resulting
wealth with those barred from the competition as partial compensation.
Thus, real estate developers get restrictive zoning laws to jack up the
prices of their homes, and then get forced to pay for some "low-income"
"public housing."

I don't see this as a good thing, rather the opposite.

